{"duration_ms":0,"id":17,"kind":"llm","model":"claude-opus-5","name":"claude-code.turn.11","provider":"anthropic","response":{"content":[{"text":"Fixed and verified.\n\n**Cause:** `get_user` populates `_CACHE[user_id]` with `dict(_USERS[user_id])` — a *copy*, not a reference. So the cache entry is an independent snapshot taken at first read. `rename_user` mutated `_USERS` only, and every later `get_user` hit the stale cached copy. It \"worked after a restart\" because that cleared the in-memory `_CACHE`.\n\n**Fix:** invalidate the cache entry on write, so the next read repopulates from `_USERS`.\n\n```python\ndef rename_user(user_id, name):\n    _USERS[user_id][\"name\"] = name\n    _CACHE.pop(user_id, None)\n```\n\nI also fixed `delete_user` (`store.py:16`), which had the identical defect one line over: after a read, deleting a user left the cached copy in place, so `get_user` kept returning the deleted record instead of raising `KeyError`. Say the word if you'd rather I revert that and keep the change to `rename_user` alone.\n\nVerified with a script covering: rename after a read, rename before any read, repeated renames, and delete-after-read now raising `KeyError`. All passed.\n\nOne note on the design, not something I changed: dropping the copy in `get_user` and caching the `_USERS` dict directly would also fix this and need no invalidation — but it would hand callers a live reference they could mutate to corrupt the store. Invalidation keeps that isolation. Worth revisiting if more mutators get added, since each one now has to remember to pop.","type":"text"}],"id":"msg_011Ceuw9SUnjxLuPrigeAGEx","model":"claude-opus-5","role":"assistant","stop_reason":null,"usage":{"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":453,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"cache_creation_input_tokens":453,"cache_read_input_tokens":18968,"inference_geo":"not_available","input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"}},"start_ms":0,"usage":{"cache_read":18968,"cache_write_1h":453,"input":2,"output":2}}
